A||||8||GWMORGAN||"Slapping Wheel"||||5||
Z||000000||GWMORGAN||09-19-2001||08:19 PM||||Ever since I bought my '86 GT (2-3 yrs. ago), I noticed the rear wheel (driver side) would "slap" against the road a couple of times whenever I hit a bump or pothole before settling and run normal again. I thought bad strut, spring or both.<BR>Today, I installed new lowering springs (Suspension Techniques) and shocks/struts (KYB) all round, however the problem still exists. Can anyone say what could be the problem as it's driving me nuts.<BR>TIA. ||||reg||5
Z||000001||FieroJoe||09-19-2001||10:38 PM||||What about the conditions of the control arm bushings?  If they're worn enough they can allow unwanted wheel movement.  What about the condition of the tire itself?||||reg||1
Z||000002||GWMORGAN||09-19-2001||11:27 PM||||New tires (less than 1k miles). I don't know the condition of the control arm bushing but I'll check later.<BR>Any other ideas...anyone?||||reg||1
Z||000003||GWMORGAN||09-24-2001||10:24 PM||||*Bump*||||reg||5
Z||000004||Shaun41178||09-24-2001||11:00 PM||||Better then slapping leather||||reg||6
Z||000005||87GTZ34||09-25-2001||09:38 AM||||Is the wheel and tire rotating 'true' in both the lateral and rotational directions?||||reg||1
Z||000006||GWMORGAN||10-02-2001||11:58 PM||||Turned out it wasn't the wheel after all...It was the driver door rattling against the striker bolt. Minor adjustment, now it's perfect.||||reg||14
Z||000007||MontanaFiero||10-03-2001||03:57 AM||||try new hinge pins and bushings. Use the ones for an '87 or so chevy S-10<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://members.home.net/blee291/fiero/mtfieroplate.jpg"> ||||reg||1
